{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[Am]Virgil [C/G]Cain is my name and I [F]served on the [Em]Danville [Dm]train,
[Am]Till Stonewall's [C/G]cavalry came and [F]tore up the [Em]tracks [Dm]again.
[C/E]In the winter of [F]sixty-five we were [C]hungry, just [Dm]barely alive.
[C/E]By May the tenth [F]Richmond had fell.
[C]It was a time I [Dm]remember all so [D]welllllllllll.[D]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
The [C/G]night they [F]drove old Dixie [C/G]down,
And the [F]bells were ringing.
The [C/G]night they [F]drove old Dixie [C/G]down,
And the [F]people were singing.  They went...
[C/G]"La la la [Am]la la la, [G]la la la la la [F]la la la la."
Intro Riff / turnaround
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Back with my wife in Tennessee, when one day she called to me.
"Virgil, quick!  Come see!  There goes Robert E. Lee!"
Now I don't mind I'm chopping wood,
And I don't care if the money's no good.
You take what you need and you leave the rest,
But they should never have taken the very best.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Like my father before me, I work the land.
And like my brother above me, who took a rebel stand.
He was just eighteen, proud and brave,
But a Yankee laid him in his grave.
I swear my the mud below my feet,
You can't raise the cane back up when it's in the feed.
{end_of_chorus}

